{"name":"Alpha Male","hex":"#715a45","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"alpha male","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The dominant male in a group of animals.","A dominant, assertive, protective, oftentimes successful, leadership-type man or male. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}"]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exhibiting the characteristics of a dominant male; dominant, assertive, and protective."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/alpha_male"}],"entries":[{"title":"Alpha Male (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Alpha Male is a 2006 American-British drama film directed by Dan Wilde and starring Jennifer Ehle and Danny Huston."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/8/8c/Alpha_male.jpg/330px-Alpha_male.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha_Male_(film)"},{"title":"Dominance hierarchy","blocks":[{"text":"In the zoological field of ethology, a dominance hierarchy is a type of social hierarchy that arises when members of animal social groups interact, creating a ranking system. Different types of interactions can result in dominance depending on the species, including ritualized displays of aggression or direct physical violence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Mandrillus_sphinx_%28alpha_male%29.jpg/330px-Mandrillus_sphinx_%28alpha_male%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dominance_hierarchy"},{"title":"Alpha","blocks":[{"text":"Alpha ALF-ə is the first letter of the Greek alphabet. In the system of Greek numerals, it has a value of one. Alpha is derived from the Phoenician letter aleph ⟨𐤀⟩, whose name comes from the West Semitic word for 'ox'. Letters that arose from alpha include the Latin letter ⟨A⟩ and the Cyrillic letter ⟨А⟩."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alpha"},{"title":"Male","blocks":[{"text":"Male is the sex of an organism that produces, or is organized to produce, the gamete known as sperm, which fuses with the larger female gamete, or ovum, in the process of fertilisation. A male organism cannot reproduce sexually without access to at least one ovum from a female, but some organisms can reproduce both sexually and asexually. Most male mammals, including male humans, have a Y chromosome, which codes for the production of larger amounts of testosterone to develop male reproductive organs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/32/Male_symbol_%28bold%2C_color%29.svg/330px-Male_symbol_%28bold%2C_color%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Male"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Weeper_Capuchin_01_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Weeper_Capuchin_01_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Weeper_Capuchin_01_%28cropped%29.JPG/1280px-Weeper_Capuchin_01_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Weeper_Capuchin_01_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Weeper_Capuchin_01_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wedge-capped capuchins have a clear dominance hierarchy.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWeeper_Capuchin_01_(cropped).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Wikipedia_project_hormone.png/500px-Wikipedia_project_hormone.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Wikipedia_project_hormone.png/500px-Wikipedia_project_hormone.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"